00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.326
- Yeah. He's dead now because
I think he's going into V-fib.

00:09:47.326 --> 00:09:48.850
I don't know what he cut.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.410
I don't know what he's doing.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:51.810
All this is unethical.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.250
Non-medically accurate.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.680
This is a joke.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:56.230
- No, no. Put him down up here.

00:09:59.095 --> 00:10:00.480
- What happened?

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.250
- There was an accident, fell off a cliff.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:04.990
- In situations like that, in a trauma,

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.960
you really want to rule
out internal bleeding,

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.840
because if a person's bleeding internally,

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.530
they are going to have moments to live.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.820
If that, because you
need to deliver fluids,

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.010
you need to deliver blood.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.820
And if you can't do that
on an Island, I don't know,

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.770
like you have no shot basically.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:18.980
- I might need some water,

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:20.693
shirts, towels, anything that I can use to

00:10:20.693 --> 00:10:21.920
stop the bleeding.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.620
- I mean, if you're
bleeding on the outside,

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.150
odds are you're bleeding from the inside,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:26.500
especially with a fall like that.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.230
So towels aren't going
to do you much good.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.130
- His lung just collapsed.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:31.573
- How does he know that?

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:34.810
He doesn't have a stethoscope.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:36.670
But if you put his ear to the chest,

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.000
or he saw that it wasn't
rising symmetrically,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:39.980
maybe I'll buy it.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.170
Maybe I'll buy it.

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:44.010
My guy looked at his
face and said his lung

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.163
collapsed. Jack has x-ray vision.

00:10:47.252 --> 00:10:50.490
- Oh it burns.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.620
- Closed fracture. Think we can set it.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:56.890
- If his leg is turning blue,
which it looked like it was,

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.509
you really should do a
reduction where you put the bone

00:10:59.509 --> 00:11:01.105
in its correct location.

00:11:01.105 --> 00:11:04.905
Reason being is if the bone is
actively blocking circulation

00:11:04.905 --> 00:11:06.776
to the lower foot, once you correct it,

00:11:06.776 --> 00:11:09.588
you can get a reperfusion
injury and die just as

00:11:09.588 --> 00:11:11.063
a result of that.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.740
- Who's checking his
cervical spine for fractures?

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.260
There's just like tossing around his neck.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:18.110
- He needs blood.

00:11:18.993 --> 00:11:19.826
- What?

00:11:19.826 --> 00:11:22.610
- Blood. Transfusion.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.880
- I don't know how he's
going to do a makeshift I V.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:26.513
- I was getting you this.

00:11:26.513 --> 00:11:27.523
- A sea urchin?

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.353
- A hollow needle, sharpen
up the Pearson needle.

00:11:34.567 --> 00:11:35.923
- Aren't those poisonous?

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.103
- It's perfect.

00:11:40.103 --> 00:11:41.262
- What I don't understand is
what is he planning on doing

00:11:41.262 --> 00:11:42.730
with this sea urchin spike?

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.630
Like he said just gonna just
spike the vein and then what?

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.140
- These types of transfusions
I've never seen work

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.380
before. I can't imagine
that it's actually working.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.560
Also, I have no idea as
he's doing this transfusion,

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.240
how we know that he's
delivered enough blood.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.740
How does he know how much
blood he's delivering?

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:06.830
I'm so used to ordering it
through a computer system,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:08.120
not just from like, all right,

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.716
let me give him some
adequate amount of blood

00:12:10.716 --> 00:12:13.190
until I guess I get dizzy and I pass out,

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:16.300
like I don't understand
how he's measuring this.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:17.250
- It's not working.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.520
- Blood's not the problem, it's the leg.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.391
All the blood's pooling there.

00:12:25.391 --> 00:12:26.893
Compartment syndrome.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.305
He didn't get this injury from a fall,

00:12:30.305 --> 00:12:32.170
something crushed his leg.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.000
- Compartment syndrome
is really problematic

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.090
because when you have a crush
injury or really any kind

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:39.299
of issue where you're
getting intense inflammation

00:12:39.299 --> 00:12:41.456
over a limb and it's circumferential

00:12:41.456 --> 00:12:43.670
meaning that it happens
all around the limb.

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:45.400
It actually starts expanding and cutting

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.530
off circulation where
necrosis starts to have it.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.321
And the treatment for this is

00:12:49.321 --> 00:12:50.640
a fasciotomy where you actually go in

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:51.789
and make a cut in order to open

00:12:51.789 --> 00:12:53.722
and allow the swelling to come out

00:12:53.722 --> 00:12:56.410
so that the pressure
gets off of the arteries

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:57.791
and allows blood to flow again.

00:12:57.791 --> 00:13:00.041
(coughing)

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.390
Again, this is surprise to no one.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:08.132
He has internal injuries.

00:13:08.132 --> 00:13:10.600
He's bleeding internally.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:12.240
Keep pumping him full of blood.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.320
Great, that maybe buys him some time.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:15.825
But the reality is, as soon
as you pump it into him,

00:13:15.825 --> 00:13:18.490
he bleeds it right back out internally.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:21.550
This is why, if anyone ever
has an abdominal injury,

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.770
like let's say a
stabbing, a gunshot wound,

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:25.930
you literally have to
open up their abdomen,

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:27.810
and run their bowels
where you're making sure

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.034
that there's no area that
your patient is bleeding

00:13:30.034 --> 00:13:32.170
out. Because if your patient bleeds out,

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:33.550
and you close them and
you think you're good,

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.200
and they continue bleeding, they can die.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.805
- But I need you to push.

00:13:38.805 --> 00:13:39.638
Okay?

00:13:40.999 --> 00:13:41.832
- Okay.

00:13:42.741 --> 00:13:44.363
- On the count of three.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:46.410
Ready?

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.660
One, two, three, push.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.150
Yeah, go ahead.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:53.780
- You want to time to pushes
with the contractions,

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.520
so when the mom is starting
to feel the contraction

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.000
come on, you ask, you confirm,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.060
and then you do a push with a count to 10.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:01.430
And then once the contractions
over, you let the mom relax.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:03.990
When the contraction comes
back, you repeat that process.

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.900
- I can see, I can see
the top of the head.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:08.333
- The baby is crowning.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.480
And it's ideal at the baby's
coming out head first.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:12.590
If the baby's coming out breach,

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:14.720
that could be very problematic.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.387
(woman pushing)

00:14:20.112 --> 00:14:23.527
- Get the shoulders out,
get the shoulders out.

00:14:23.527 --> 00:14:24.694
One at a time.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.227
There's a baby. That is not a new born.

00:14:28.227 --> 00:14:29.884
(laughs)

00:14:29.884 --> 00:14:32.270
Classic with the baby's crying.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:35.163
Good color, probably good Apgar scores.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.520
Getting the baby right on
mom's chest, perfect form.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:41.730
I like it.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.580
Real doctor versus TV doctor.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:44.440
I actually share a lot

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.570
of my really interesting
doctor patient stories.

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.020
So click on that and as always,

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.223
stay happy and healthy.

00:14:50.307 --> 00:14:53.807
(electronic bounce music)

